---
import Doc from "../../layouts/Doc.astro";
import MethodologyNav from "../../components/MethodologyNav.astro";
import MethodFacts from "../../components/MethodFacts.astro";
import AxisMeter from "../../components/AxisMeter.astro";
import VoidHatch from "../../components/VoidHatch.astro";
import { AXES, M, EVIDENCE_CAP, percent, stackingPercents, stackingTailPercent } from "../../lib/methodology";

const howScored: Record<string, string> = {
  code_safety: "Opens at 100; each semgrep finding deducts by severity and confidence.",
  auth_posture: "Weighted sub-checks: authentication model, transport, secrets, authorization.",
  dependency_health: "Opens at 100; each known vulnerability deducts by severity and whether it is direct.",
  maintenance: "Weighted sub-checks: recency, issue response, release cadence, bus factor.",
  transparency: "Weighted sub-checks: licence, README, declared scopes, changelog, security contact.",
};
const critical = M.code_safety_deductions[0];
---

<Doc
  title="Methodology — mcpwatchman"
  description="How mcpwatchman scores MCP servers: five independent axes, every weight and deduction, why the composite is withheld, and what an unassessed axis means."
  canonical="https://mcpwatchman.com/methodology/"
  heading="How a score is made"
>
  <Fragment slot="lede">
    <p>
      mcpwatchman reads what an MCP server publishes — source, manifests,
      lockfiles, repository history — and scores it on five axes, each 0 to
      100 and each on its own. Nothing here is hidden: the rules, the weights
      and the arithmetic are in the public repository, and this section
      prints the numbers the engine actually uses.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
  <MethodFacts slot="facts" />

  <MethodologyNav />
  <VoidHatch />

  <figure class="reading" aria-labelledby="reading-caption">
    <ul class="legend">
      <li>
        <svg width="22" height="10" viewBox="0 0 22 10" aria-hidden="true"><rect class="m-scored" width="22" height="10" rx="1"></rect></svg>
        scored
      </li>
      <li>
        <svg width="22" height="10" viewBox="0 0 22 10" aria-hidden="true"><rect class="m-lost" width="22" height="10" rx="1"></rect></svg>
        deducted
      </li>
      <li>
        <svg width="22" height="10" viewBox="0 0 22 10" aria-hidden="true"><rect class="m-void" width="22" height="10" rx="1" fill="url(#void-hatch)"></rect></svg>
        never measured
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="rail">
      <AxisMeter axis="example-full" label="Fully measured" score={72} assessedWeight="1" />
      <AxisMeter axis="example-partial" label="Partly measured" score={80} assessedWeight="0.25" />
      <AxisMeter axis="example-none" label="Not assessed" score={null} assessedWeight="0" />
    </div>
    <figcaption id="reading-caption">
      How to read every meter on this site (illustrations, not servers). The
      track is the whole axis. The middle meter is the case this design exists
      for: a server whose repository cannot be read can still score 80 on the
      one Auth Posture sub-check that needs only its registry manifest — 80 of
      a quarter of the axis, which is not 80. The unmeasured share is hatched,
      because a grey fill reads as zero and a hatch reads as absence.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <section class="paper doc-paper" aria-labelledby="axes-heading">
    <h2 id="axes-heading">Five axes, scored independently</h2>
    <p>
      Each axis has its own page with its full table. The weights are what the
      composite would use; they are provisional starting values, not
      calibration outputs, and they are not applied to anything published.
    </p>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table class="ledger">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Axis</th>
            <th scope="col" class="num">Provisional weight</th>
            <th scope="col">How it is scored</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {AXES.map((a) => (
            <tr>
              <th scope="row"><a href={`/methodology/${a.slug}/`}>{a.label}</a></th>
              <td class="num">{percent(a.weight)}%</td>
              <td>{howScored[a.key]}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="doc-section" aria-labelledby="composite-heading">
    <h2 id="composite-heading">There is no overall score, yet</h2>
    <p>
      The methodology defines a composite: the weighted sum of the five axis
      scores, rounded half up. The engine computes it, and no page, API record,
      CLI output or badge publishes it. It stays withheld until a hand-audited
      gold set shows that the weights rank servers the way a security engineer
      would.
    </p>
    <p>
      That gold set is not being built. A hand audit of thirty servers is more
      than this project can staff, so no entry is being ratified, and what a
      ratified set would license — a published composite, and rules claiming
      high confidence — stays withheld rather than being licensed some other
      way. Drafted, unverified audit labels are still used, in one direction
      only: they may justify narrowing a rule, demoting it or stopping it
      deducting, because a wrong label then costs a missed finding. They may
      never justify raising a rule's confidence.
    </p>
    <p>
      If you need a single number, weight the axes yourself and own that
      choice. The README badge names a server's lowest-scoring axis instead,
      the most conservative summary there is.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="doc-section" aria-labelledby="confidence-heading">
    <h2 id="confidence-heading">No rule claims high confidence</h2>
    <p>
      A finding's confidence is defined as a measured false-positive rate
      against the gold set: high means under 5%, medium under 20%. Nobody has
      performed that measurement, so no rule may claim high. The engine
      enforces the ceiling — every finding is capped at{" "}
      <strong>{M.confidence_ceiling}</strong> — rather than trusting rule
      authors to remember it.
    </p>
    <p>
      The cost runs in one direction. A critical finding at high confidence
      would deduct {critical.high}; at medium it deducts {critical.medium}.
      Every server currently scores better than it would after calibration,
      and under-accusing while uncalibrated is the correct way to be wrong.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="doc-section" aria-labelledby="unassessed-heading">
    <h2 id="unassessed-heading">Unassessed is not zero</h2>
    <p>
      When a sub-check cannot be evaluated — no source to read, no lockfile,
      a band the methodology does not define — it is left out of the axis
      and its weight is renormalised away. Scoring it 0 would accuse a server
      of a fault nobody looked for; scoring it 100 would hide risk behind a
      number that looks measured.
    </p>
    <p>
      So every score travels with its <code>assessed_weight</code>: the share
      of the axis that was actually measured, from 0 to 1, rounded down so an
      incomplete measurement never reads as a complete one. A score means
      “of what we could measure, this”. An axis with nothing measurable has a
      score of <code>null</code>, which is the opposite claim to 0: not
      assessed, rather than assessed and failed.
    </p>
    <p>
      Where the methodology defines no band for a case, the scanner abstains
      instead of inventing one.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="doc-section" aria-labelledby="fault-heading">
    <h2 id="fault-heading">Whose gap it is</h2>
    <p>
      An unassessed axis also says why, and whose limit the gap is. The{" "}
      <code>fault</code> field takes one of four values:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>publisher</strong> — a property of what the server published:
        an unreachable repository, no lockfile, no source in a language the
        ruleset covers. A fact its page owes the reader. GitHub answers 404
        for a private repository and an absent one alike, so this means
        “we could not read it”, never “it does not exist”.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>project</strong> — a limit of mcpwatchman, stated in our own
        voice: a check we have not built, a band the methodology leaves
        undefined, a bounded fetch that stopped short.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>environment</strong> — an accident of one run, such as a
        scanner that timed out. Never published.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>unattributed</strong> — the default when nothing recorded a
        cause. Never published.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The last two are refused at the point of publication, so a gap caused by
      our own tooling cannot reach a page as a statement about someone else's
      server. A pinned server whose rescan failed for our reasons keeps its
      previous page, labelled as such. The refusal also reaches inside scored
      axes: an axis can publish a number while one of its sub-checks
      abstained, and <code>unmeasured_faults</code> lists whose those
      abstentions were.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="doc-section" aria-labelledby="evidence-heading">
    <h2 id="evidence-heading">Every point traces to evidence</h2>
    <p>
      Every deduction names the artefact that produced it — a file and line
      with the matched code for a rule, an advisory ID for a vulnerability, a
      date or count for a maintenance signal. A finding whose evidence cannot
      be linked does not appear.
    </p>
    <p>
      A page shows at most the {EVIDENCE_CAP} most severe findings per axis
      and says so when it stops. The score is computed over all of them; the
      cap limits what is printed, not what is counted.
    </p>
    <p>
      Findings in test code are listed, marked, and deduct nothing: an axis
      grades the code a user runs.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="doc-section" aria-labelledby="arithmetic-heading">
    <h2 id="arithmetic-heading">The arithmetic</h2>
    <p>
      The two deduction-scored axes share one stacking rule. Findings of the
      same severity stack with diminishing returns, largest first: the first
      takes {stackingPercents[0]}% of its deduction, then{" "}
      {stackingPercents.slice(1).join("%, ")}%, and {stackingTailPercent}% from
      the {stackingPercents.length + 1}th on. Twenty critical findings and one
      do not score the same, and the worst finding still takes the full hit.
    </p>
    <p>
      Arithmetic is exact decimal, never floating point, and only the
      published axis score is rounded — half up, at the end. Rounding a
      deduction first would turn half-up into half-down on the number a
      reader sees.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="doc-section" aria-labelledby="versions-heading">
    <h2 id="versions-heading">Versions</h2>
    <p>
      Every server page and API record names the methodology version that
      produced it, and the ruleset version wherever a rule ran. The methodology is at{" "}
      <strong>{M.methodology_version}</strong>. New rules and rule fixes are a
      patch release; weight changes, new sub-checks and formula changes a
      minor one; adding or removing an axis a major one. The semgrep ruleset is
      versioned separately, and every change to it is recorded, with the
      measurement behind it, in the{" "}
      <a href="https://github.com/kvadrum/mcpwatchman/blob/main/rules/_meta/changelog.md">ruleset changelog</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="doc-section" aria-labelledby="limits-heading">
    <h2 id="limits-heading">What this methodology does not capture</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Behaviour under hostile input.</strong> The analysis is static:
        source is read, never run, and no running server is contacted. Clean
        static analysis does not rule out runtime misbehaviour.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Tampering between repository and package.</strong> We do not
        verify that a published package was built from the repository it
        declares.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A compromised or coerced maintainer.</strong> Static analysis
        cannot see it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Whether a server does what it claims.</strong> We check that
        it does what it does safely, not that it is correct.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Privacy.</strong> Outbound calls can be detected; whether they
        leak sensitive data is not assessed.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Who is behind a commit.</strong> Bus factor counts commit
        identities, so an AI agent committing under its own identity counts as
        an author, as a person does.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      A score is a summary of measurable signals at a point in time, not a
      recommendation. We do not say “do not install this server”; we say what
      we found and where.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="doc-section" aria-labelledby="prior-heading">
    <h2 id="prior-heading">Prior art</h2>
    <p>
      The published-rubric posture follows Mozilla Observatory; the
      multi-check shape is closest to OpenSSF Scorecard; the per-finding
      evidence trail follows Snyk; severity bands use CVSS v3.1. Widely quoted
      figures on MCP vulnerability prevalence come from vendor research with
      unpublished methods. We cite them as the reason to build an axis, never
      as a baseline our scores are calibrated or compared against.
    </p>
  </section>
</Doc>
